"Each spring, hawks called gray-faced buzzards appear over Bagua Mountain in Changhua. They have flown from Southeast Asia, and they are on their way to nest in Japan, Korea, and northeastern China. In autumn they will fly back. The round trip is about 9,000 kilometers, and they make it every year.": "b4033cc156dd",
"Each spring, hawks called gray-faced buzzards appear over Bagua Mountain in Changhua. They have flown from Southeast Asia, and they are on their way to nest in Japan, Korea, and northeastern China. In autumn they will fly back. The round trip is about 9,000 kilometers, and they make it every year. They are not the only ones. About 1,800 of the world's 10,000 kinds of birds are long-distance migrants. How do they find the way? People have studied this question for a long time, and the answer is that birds use several clues at once. The first clue is the Sun. But the Sun moves across the sky all day, so its direction means nothing unless you also know the time. Birds have a body clock, as we do, and they use it together with the Sun to work out direction. At night the Sun is gone, and many birds migrate at night. They steer by the stars. In experiments, birds kept under the artificial stars of a planetarium turned to face the direction in which they would normally migrate. The third clue works even under thick cloud. The Earth is a giant magnet, and birds can sense its magnetic field. Scientists showed this in 1972. They are still working out how the sense works, and there is evidence that it involves a light-sensitive protein in the bird's eye. The last clue is memory. Birds learn coasts, rivers, and mountains, and they recognize them again. Young storks learn the route by following older ones. But in some kinds of birds the young set off alone, in the right direction, on a journey they have never made. That direction was inherited from their parents.": "11efce2be912",
